WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:06.160
A deleted demon, creepy motel visitors, and
a post-credits teaser setting up the next

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.049
Conjuring movie are some of the many scenes
cut from The Devil Made Me Do It.

00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:15.160
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing the fascinating scenes,

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.140
original ending and villain cut from The Conjuring
3!

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.150
Spoilers for the franchise ahead, so take
care.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.960
In a departure from the haunted house theme
of the first two Conjuring movies, The Devil

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.869
Made Me Do It is more of a demonic detective
story with Ed and Lorraine searching for clues

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:33.110
that will eventually lead them to the Occultist's
underground lair.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:37.180
However, in the trailers, there's a good deal
of footage that points to an alternate storyline

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.800
for how the Warrens discover the Occultist's
den.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.580
Notice how the trailer shows Ed and Lorraine
opening the door together on her altar, compared

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.440
to the final movie where Lorraine finds the
room by herself after she goes to visit Kastner

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.590
alone and he reveals to her the truth about
his daughter and the tunnels.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:58.410
In this earlier version of the movie, the
location also looks quite a bit different.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:02.410
There's what looks like a tree behind the
altar, plus an animal which could be a goat

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:07.421
or perhaps even a ram, given we know the Occultist
developed her interest in Satanism from the

00:01:07.421 --> 00:01:11.439
objects her father collected while investigating
the Disciples of the Ram.

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:15.390
Ed also carries a package with him into the
room which he uses to sweep everything off

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.090
the Occultist's altar in this deleted scene.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:22.509
In the final movie, Ed doesn't arrive at the
altar room until the very end when he's under

00:01:22.509 --> 00:01:25.429
the influence of the Occultist and is attacking
Lorraine.

00:01:25.429 --> 00:01:29.840
And after Lorraine breaks him out of the trance,
he smashes the altar with a sledgehammer.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.740
Some behind-the-scenes footage also shows
another different version of Lorraine going

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.109
to the altar room by herself than we get in
the finished movie.

00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:41.469
We know this was deleted because Lorraine's
hair is up in this shot, rather than down

00:01:41.469 --> 00:01:45.899
as it is in the final film at this point,
and she's also got a bandage on her hand.

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:49.539
An intriguing deleted scene teased in the
trailer, but which never made it into the

00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:54.409
final film, is of Ed and Lorraine during their
stay at a motel while investigating the Jessica

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:55.700
and Katie case.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.819
In this cut scene, Ed is on the phone, but
Lorraine has been distracted by something,

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:04.279
a row of creepy shadows outside the motel
window, which feel like they're looking in

00:02:04.279 --> 00:02:05.409
on the Warrens.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:09.250
You can tell this is a motel and not the Warren
house, because of the Do Not Disturb sign

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:13.170
on the door knob, and I think this could have
been another of the optical illusions that

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.460
the Occultist is able to perform.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.599
A particularly cool moment teased in the trailers
which sadly didn't make it into the final

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:24.790
film was this shot of multiple copies of Lorraine
endlessly spiralling into the distance.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.459
This would have been a visual trick cast by
the Occultist while she was pursuing Lorraine

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:29.730
through the tunnels.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.989
This moment was foreshadowed during the opening
scenes of David's exorcism, when the camera

00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:35.980
that Drew was filming with was knocked over.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.629
As the camera continued to record the monitor
it was connected to, an image of the screen

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:45.599
began to replicate itself infinitely, in a
very similar pattern to what happens to Lorraine

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:47.099
in this deleted scene.

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:51.980
In the final movie, we get a kind of cut-down,
somewhat simplified version of this trippy

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:56.590
visual, with a double of Lorraine appearing
in front of her and mirroring her movements.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.400
Although this was still a nice effect that
suitably freaked Lorraine out, it's a shame

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.829
we didn't get the full-on sequence teased
at the beginning of the movie.

00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:08.020
Another deleted scene from the trailers are
these shots of the police searching for a

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.020
body.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.569
This is the water below the cliff that Lorraine
nearly fell down while she was investigating

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:14.900
Katie's murder at the hands of Jessica.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.380
These scenes were probably cut for pacing
and time in the final version;

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:22.439
however, they might also be another indicator
of reshoots in the movie, because it could

00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:27.510
explain why in the final film, a bandage suddenly
appears on Lorraine's hand after she almost

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:32.280
falls off the cliff, even though she didn't
seem to hurt her hand when Ed rescued her.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.989
Although it is possible it's an injury from
that fall, it might hint that something else

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:38.870
happened to Lorraine during these deleted
scenes.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:42.900
A curious deleted scene in one of the trailers
shows Arne standing on the edge of a prison

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.510
building with a chain around his neck, looking
like he might be about to kill himself.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:52.299
In the movie, the Occultist tries to get Arne
to commit suicide, like Jessica when she ran

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:56.319
off the cliff, in order to complete the curse
she cast on him.

00:03:56.319 --> 00:04:00.310
Some fans were a little disappointed that
The Devil Made Me Do It didn't dive so much

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:04.590
into the details of Arne Johnson's court trial,
and although I doubt it was ever going to

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.939
be the main focus, there's some behind-the-scenes
footage of Ed addressing reporters outside

00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:13.340
the courthouse which wasn't in the final movie,
hinting that there may have been more courtroom

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:16.359
drama filmed but cut from the finished film.

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:21.400
A publicity still of Lorraine, Ed, Drew and
Debbie forming a prayer circle indicates another

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.460
scene that was nowhere to be found in the
final movie.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.930
This moment looks like it would have appeared
around the time that the Warrens and the team

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:32.389
are trying to figure out the key to breaking
the curse put on Arne by the Occultist, and

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:35.110
I suspect the prayer was cut for time.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:39.120
A major change that might explain why there
appear to have been reshoots on the film is

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.199
that in early versions of the movie there
was a specific demon that the Occultist was

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:44.560
communicating with.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.150
In the final film, although a reference is
made to the Occultist having summoned a demon,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.620
"This demon...
it's not here of its own free will.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:52.620
It was summoned."

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:56.930
we never see its form and instead at the end,
when the curse has been broken, it's a copy

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.500
of the Occultist which comes to take her soul.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:04.020
In an interview with SlashFilm, director Michael
Chaves said that originally they had a demon

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.349
that matched the description that the real-life
David Glatzel and Arne Johnson had given to

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:11.569
them about the demonic entity they claimed
had possessed them.

00:05:11.569 --> 00:05:15.610
Producer James Wan was on board with the demon,
and a cut of the film with the demon does

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:16.720
in fact exist.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.810
However, it appears that the interaction between
the demon and the Occultist was too complicated

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:26.840
and confusing for test audiences which Chaves
puts down to this being the first time a human

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.960
antagonist has featured in the franchise.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.500
At the time of filming, Chaves was actually
expecting the demon to end up with his own

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.190
spin-off film,
but admitted that it just didn't click and

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:40.610
had to be replaced in a similar way to how
the original antagonist of The Conjuring 2,

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:46.460
a winged, horned demon ended up being replaced
by the demonic nun Valak during reshoots.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:50.920
Because Chaves felt bad for the actor Davis
Osborne who'd played the original demon, he

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.850
decided to give him the part of the prison
hospital patient who mocks and taunts Arne.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.889
The Devil Made Me Do It also originally had
a post-credits scene that would have set up

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:02.130
a future Conjuring Universe movie.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:06.580
In an interview with NME, Chaves revealed
that the deleted credits teaser was an expansion

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:10.889
on the villains introduced in this movie;
however, similar to the demon problem, it

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:15.620
was confusing and therefore removed, to give
the film a more solid, definitive ending.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:19.830
Chaves hasn't yet said what was in the scene
but I wonder whether it could have alluded

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:24.580
to the return of Valak in a fourth Conjuring
movie, or perhaps a sequel to The Nun?

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:29.270
There were already a few teases to Valak in
the film, such as the nun figurine in Arne's

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:32.270
bedroom,
but as TITAN BR pointed out on my Things You

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:36.500
Missed video, there was also this photograph
of the Occultist when she was younger and

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:40.550
in the background towards the window, there
seems to be a mysterious shape which could

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:45.210
be a blurry and somewhat indistinct or semi-transparent
Nun's habit.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:50.039
The date on the photograph is 1952 which is
the year that the main events of The Nun take

00:06:50.039 --> 00:06:54.690
place, something which suggests the filmmakers
want to tease a potential connection between

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:55.940
the two movies.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:00.020
We have had the demonic nun hidden in the
background of a photo before in the franchise,

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:05.040
for example in the photo that Sister Charlotte
showed to Samuel Mullins in Annabelle Creation.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.490
Perhaps this post-credits scene would have
shown the Occultist having been previously

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:11.139
in contact with Valak in some way.

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:16.039
Another possibility is that she was visited
by Frenchie who in 1952 was possessed by Valak

00:07:16.039 --> 00:07:18.150
during the events of The Nun movie.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:22.349
Alternately, the credits scene might have
expanded on the connection between the Occultist

00:07:22.349 --> 00:07:26.289
and the Disciples of the Ram, the cult from
the first Annabelle movie that was mentioned

00:07:26.289 --> 00:07:28.789
several times in The Devil Made Me Do It.

00:07:28.789 --> 00:07:33.389
We know from Father Kastner that the Disciples
of the Ram were especially evil and vindictive

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:37.659
and that after the cults' members were tried
and found guilty, the lead prosecutor's wife

00:07:37.659 --> 00:07:40.050
and baby both suffered terrible fates.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:44.659
If any of the cult are still around or if
they can break out of prison, they might target

00:07:44.659 --> 00:07:49.069
Ed and Lorraine for helping to take down the
Occultist, something that could be the set-up

00:07:49.069 --> 00:07:51.409
for the next Conjuring or Annabelle movie.

00:07:51.409 --> 00:07:54.860
"A master Satanist is not an adversary to
be taken lightly."

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:59.330
The credits scene might also have been setting
up the Crooked Man spin-off movie that's currently

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:04.169
in development based on the villain from the
haunted turntable in the Conjuring 2.

00:08:04.169 --> 00:08:08.220
Whatever the scene entails, we'll hopefully
get to see it as Chaves said the scene may

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:12.790
be coming back in another form, hinting that
it'll be worked into an upcoming movie or

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:16.580
perhaps even as a deleted clip for the movie's
Blu-ray release.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:20.169
And if you want to learn all about how The
Devil Made Me Do It connects with the Nun

00:08:20.169 --> 00:08:24.389
and the Annabelle movies plus all the horror
